<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fructuoso Rivera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a>–<a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fructuoso Rivera was a Uruguayan caudillo and soldier who became the first constitutional president of the Oriental Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> in 1830. A veteran of Artigas's struggles and the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> party, and his rivalry with Manuel Oribe and the Blancos led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war">Guerra Grande</a> of 1839 to 1851. He served as president again from 1839 to 1843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera.jpg" alt="Fructuoso Rivera" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Manuel Blanes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Manuel_Blanes_-_Retrato_del_General_Fructuoso_Rivera.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rivera was a rancher who joined the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a> in 1810 and eventually rose to the rank of general. When the Banda Oriental was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a> and Artigas was forced into exile in 1820, Rivera stayed in the newly created Cisplatina province and briefly served in the Imperial Brazilian Army, reaching the rank of brigadier.</p><p>He first met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-antonio-lavalleja">Juan Antonio Lavalleja</a> in 1825, in an event known as the Abrazo del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a>. In the same year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-three-orientals">Thirty-Three Orientals</a> led by Lavalleja and their Argentine supporters began their fight against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a>, and Rivera joined them, though it is unclear whether he did so voluntarily. He became an important commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war">Cisplatine War</a> and took part in the Battle of Rincón and the Battle of Sarandí.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After Uruguayan independence was proclaimed in 1828 through the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, arguments between Rivera and Lavalleja turned into fighting, and José Rondeau became the first provisional governor. Rivera held the presidency from 6 November 1830 until 24 October 1834 and survived an unsuccessful assassination attempt and coup by Lavalleja's supporters in 1832. On 11 April 1831 he organised the Salsipuedes Massacre: Charrúa leaders and warriors were lured to a meeting and killed by the military, and surviving women and children were marched to Montevideo and held in household servitude.</p><p>Rivera backed Manuel Oribe as his successor but came into conflict with him. In October 1838 he defeated Oribe, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. Rivera's supporters wore red armbands and Oribe's white, the origin of the division between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a> and Blancos. Rivera held the presidency again from 1 March 1839 to 1 March 1843. Oribe, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, and in December 1842 he defeated Rivera at the Battle of Arroyo Grande and began the Great Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rivera's power was limited to the capital until he was forced into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1847; he stayed there until 1853. A triumvirate of Venancio Flores, Lavalleja and Rivera was created on 25 September 1853. Lavalleja died on 22 October, and Rivera died on 13 January 1854 on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>. He is regarded as the founder of the Colorado Party, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> without interruption from 1865 until 1958, and a Riverista tendency within it remains a counterpoint to the Batllista and other factions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fructuoso Rivera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new country had just under 75,000 people and two parties, the Blancos of the second president, Manuel Oribe and the Colorados of the first, <b>Fructuoso Rivera</b>, whose quarrel became the Guerra Grande in 1839.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Melo, in what is now <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-32.37,-54.17&amp;year=1829" aria-label="Melo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3158 3340 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Fructuoso Rivera"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3608 3610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3608 3610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-32.37,-54.17&amp;year=1829">What was here in 1829? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
